{
	"comment": "Shared conformance vectors for the index-entry encoding: engine/query/indexkey.go and client/src/query/indexkey.ts must produce byte-identical entries. rows are protojson syncplane.testdata.v1.Widget unless type says otherwise; spec.key paths use proto field names (Widget's localNames coincide). encodeCases pin the bytes themselves, as testdata/text does, so a change to either encoder that is not mirrored in the other fails here rather than silently corrupting stored indexes. orderCases pin the property that actually matters: sorting rows by their encoded entry must give the SAME order as the row comparator (Order.Compare / compareRows) over an ordering with the same keys, which both loaders assert against each other rather than only against `want`. probeCases pin the PLANNER's decisions — which index it picks, how many ranges it produces, whether it claims the ordering, and whether anything is left to re-check. Both planners must agree: a planner that chose differently would still return the right rows, just at a different cost, so nothing else in either suite would notice the divergence. `want` is \"scan\" when the query must fall back. Ranges are not compared byte-for-byte here — encodeCases already pin the encoding, and the bounds are derived from it. A component whose path runs through a repeated field fans out: the row takes one entry per element, an empty list still takes one entry tagged missing so every live row stays reachable, and duplicate elements collapse. Components sharing a list fan out TOGETHER, element by element — the cross-product pairing is what a co-referring query would silently get wrong, so \"two components on one list stay paired per element\" is the case that pins it.",
